From: pf <xiaofei450@126.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 01:17:50 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] Turn off Nagle on HTTP client sockets

Response.send() writes a response in two goes - it flushes the header block, then
sendBody() writes the body to the raw socket stream - and both writes are below one
MSS. nanohttpd never sets TCP_NODELAY, so Nagle withholds the body until the peer's
delayed ACK arrives.

Measured from a Windows client against this server:
  - every response paid 48-110 ms between the header segment and the body segment
  - a response body larger than MSS, which Nagle is obliged to let through,
    arrived in 0-1 ms

That contrast is what identifies the stall as Nagle rather than the network.
createClientHandler() is the only hook that can reach the accepted socket, because
nanohttpd builds it inside ServerRunnable.

diff --git a/app/src/main/java/com/atharok/btremote/presentation/http/HttpRemoteServer.kt b/app/src/main/java/com/atharok/btremote/presentation/http/HttpRemoteServer.kt
index 360c367..ac2d1b5 100644
--- a/app/src/main/java/com/atharok/btremote/presentation/http/HttpRemoteServer.kt
+++ b/app/src/main/java/com/atharok/btremote/presentation/http/HttpRemoteServer.kt
@@ -33,6 +33,8 @@ import kotlinx.serialization.json.jsonObject
 import kotlinx.serialization.json.longOrNull
 import kotlinx.serialization.json.put
 import java.io.IOException
+import java.io.InputStream
+import java.net.Socket
 import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger
 
 /**
@@ -118,6 +120,33 @@ class HttpRemoteServer(
         }
     }
 
+    // ---- Socket options ----
+
+    /**
+     * Turns off Nagle on every accepted connection.
+     *
+     * [Response.send] writes a response in two goes: it flushes the header block, then
+     * `sendBody` writes the body straight to the raw socket stream that `ClientHandler` took
+     * from `acceptSocket.getOutputStream()`. Both writes are below one MSS, so with Nagle on
+     * the second is withheld until the peer acknowledges the first - and that peer answers
+     * with a delayed ACK, tens of milliseconds later. Measured against this server from a
+     * Windows client, every response paid 48-110 ms that way, while a response body larger
+     * than MSS (which Nagle is obliged to let through) arrived in 0-1 ms. The stall is Nagle,
+     * not the network.
+     *
+     * nanohttpd never sets TCP_NODELAY, and it builds the socket inside `ServerRunnable`, so
+     * this hook is the only place that can reach it. The call is wrapped because a failure
+     * here must not cost us the response - the API keeps working either way.
+     */
+    override fun createClientHandler(finalAccept: Socket, inputStream: InputStream): ClientHandler {
+        try {
+            finalAccept.tcpNoDelay = true
+        } catch (e: Exception) {
+            Log.w(HTTP_LOG_TAG, "Could not set TCP_NODELAY on an HTTP client socket", e)
+        }
+        return super.createClientHandler(finalAccept, inputStream)
+    }
+
     // ---- Request entry point ----
 
     override fun serve(session: IHTTPSession): Response {
